{"ClientSettings":{"Pn":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"P"},"Sc":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"H"},"Qz":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"T"},"Ap":true,"Mute":true,"Lad":"2026-08-01T00:00:00Z","Iotd":0,"Gwb":0,"Tns":0,"Dft":null,"Mvs":0,"Flt":0,"Imp":1,"Tobn":0},"MediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some railways retire. The Virginia Creeper Trail simply changed gears. Stretching 55 kilometres through southwest Virginia, this trail follows a former rail line once used to haul lumber and iron ore through the Appalachian Mountains. Its unusual name traces back to the steam locomotives that laboured up steep grades in the early 1900s.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.VirginiaTrail_ROW8255476190_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.VirginiaTrail_ROW8255476190_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Aerial view of the Virginia Creeper Trail, Damascus, Virginia, United States","Copyright":"© Eifel Kreutz/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260731_VirginiaTrail\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Virginia+Creeper+Trail+Damascus&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260731_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260731_VirginiaTrail"},"Ssd":"20260731","Name":"VirginiaTrail","Market":"ROW","Hash":"8255476190","FullDateString":"31 July 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some landscapes are famous. Monument Valley is legendary. Straddling the Arizona–Utah border within the Navajo Nation, this desert wonder is shaped by towering sandstone buttes that rise as much as 305 metres above the valley floor. Known in Navajo as Tsé Bii' Ndzisgaii—'valley of the rocks'—the region blends dramatic geology with living culture.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.NavajoNation_ROW6898030800_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.NavajoNation_ROW6898030800_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Monument Valley, Navajo Nation, Arizona, United States","Copyright":"© Westend61/Adobe Stock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260730_NavajoNation\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Monument+Valley&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260730_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260730_NavajoNation"},"Ssd":"20260730","Name":"NavajoNation","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6898030800","FullDateString":"30 July 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Follow this tiger family through Ranthambore National Park in Rajasthan, India, where every step seems effortless and every shadow could hide a striped giant. These are Bengal tigers, built for both stealth and power: an adult can stretch over 3 metres from nose to tail and weigh up to 230 kilograms, yet walk without making a sound. Their distinctive stripes also play a part in a landscape where visibility is a double-edged sword, helping them blend into tall grasses and dappled forests so prey struggle to spot them.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.TigerFamily_ROW4948587422_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.TigerFamily_ROW4948587422_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"A family of Bengal tigers at Ranthambore National Park, Rajasthan, India","Copyright":"© Archna Singh/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260729_TigerFamily\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Ranthambore+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260729_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260729_TigerFamily"},"Ssd":"20260729","Name":"TigerFamily","Market":"ROW","Hash":"4948587422","FullDateString":"29 July 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Off Anacapa Island, California, USA, the ocean grows its own forests. Within Channel Islands National Park—also known as the 'Galápagos of North America'—giant kelp can stretch beyond 30 metres in length, forming buoyant ribbons lifted by air-filled bladders and anchored below by rootlike holdfasts.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ChannelKelp_ROW4859123626_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ChannelKelp_ROW4859123626_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Kelp","Title":"A kelp forest off Anacapa Island, Channel Islands National Park, California, USA","Copyright":"© Ian Shive/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260728_ChannelKelp\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Channel+Islands+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260728_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260728_ChannelKelp"},"Ssd":"20260728","Name":"ChannelKelp","Market":"ROW","Hash":"4859123626","FullDateString":"28 July 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some places are best introduced with one word: Yoho. It is a Cree expression of wonder, which is exactly the reaction most people have. Established in 1886, Yoho National Park protects part of the Canadian Rockies in British Columbia. Visitors can stop at Takakkaw Falls, where water plunges more than 370 metres, or explore the world-famous Burgess Shale fossil sites. These rocks preserve the remains of marine animals that lived more than 500 million years ago.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SummerBC_ROW3830675043_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SummerBC_ROW3830675043_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Emerald Lake, Yoho National Park, British Columbia, Canada","Copyright":"© Olga Matveeva/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260727_SummerBC\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Yoho+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260727_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260727_SummerBC"},"Ssd":"20260727","Name":"SummerBC","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3830675043","FullDateString":"27 July 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"At sunrise in Honduras' Pig Keys—small, low-lying coastal islets tucked within mangrove-rich wetlands—a red mangrove lifts its tangled roots above glassy water. Red mangroves are often the first trees to colonise tropical coastlines, using their distinctive arching roots to anchor in soft, oxygen-poor mud where saltwater and land collide. These roots help trap sediment, stabilise shorelines and create calm waters where other coastal plants can take hold. Their dense networks also reduce erosion, soften the impact of waves and help shield nearby communities from storms. Beneath the surface, the submerged roots provide shelter for young fish, crabs and shrimp, making these wetlands one of the ocean's most productive nurseries.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.RedMangroveSunrise_ROW3783408236_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.RedMangroveSunrise_ROW3783408236_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Sunrise on a red mangrove in the Pig Keys, Honduras","Copyright":"© Mac Stone/Tandem Stills + Motion","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260726_RedMangroveSunrise\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Red+mangrove&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260726_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260726_RedMangroveSunrise"},"Ssd":"20260726","Name":"RedMangroveSunrise","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3783408236","FullDateString":"26 July 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In Isla Cristina, Huelva, Spain, the Atlantic shapes a sunlit coastline where salt marshes, beaches and a rich seafaring tradition set the rhythm of daily life. From above, its wide stretches of fine sandy beach and the channels winding through the saltworks reflect the close connection between the landscape and the fishing industry, which has long defined the town's identity.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.IslaCristina_ROW3744341515_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.IslaCristina_ROW3744341515_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Isla Cristina, Huelva, Spain","Copyright":"© MEDITERRANEAN/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260725_IslaCristina\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Isla+Cristina&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260725_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260725_IslaCristina"},"Ssd":"20260725","Name":"IslaCristina","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3744341515","FullDateString":"25 July 2026"}],"PreloadMediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In Copenhagen, Nyhavn Canal is a burst of colour and history. In Denmark's capital, bright, gabled houses lean toward the water, their reflections rippling beside old wooden ships. Carved out in the 1670s under King Christian V, this 17th-century canal once served as a busy gateway where cargo vessels unloaded goods straight into the city's heart. Sailors crowded its taverns, and the harbour gained a rough reputation long before it became the postcard-perfect place seen today.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.ColorfulCop_ROW6097405388_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.ColorfulCop_ROW6097405388_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Colourful homes line Nyhavn Canal, Copenhagen, Denmark","Copyright":"© emicristea/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260807_ColorfulCop\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Nyhavn+Canal+Copenhagen&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260807_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260807_ColorfulCop"},"Ssd":"20260807","Name":"ColorfulCop","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6097405388","FullDateString":"7 Aug 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In the Masai Mara, Kenya, the ground seems to move. A vast surge of wildebeest—hoofed grazers built for endurance—travel in tightly packed herds, drawn by fresh grass and water replenished by seasonal rains.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.MaraCrossing_ROW6582779125_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.MaraCrossing_ROW6582779125_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Migrating wildebeest crossing Mara River in Masai Mara, Kenya","Copyright":"© Manoj Shah/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260806_MaraCrossing\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Wildebeest&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260806_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260806_MaraCrossing"},"Ssd":"20260806","Name":"MaraCrossing","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6582779125","FullDateString":"6 Aug 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In Fez, Morocco, the Royal Palace—known as Dar al-Makhzen—has stood as a seat of power since the 13th century, when Morocco's rulers established a fortified royal city around it. Behind its high walls lie gardens, courtyards, mosques and royal residences spread across a vast complex still used by the king today, making it both historic and fully active.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.FezMorocco_ROW6564333571_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.FezMorocco_ROW6564333571_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Decorated gate of the Royal Palace of Fez, Morocco","Copyright":"© cgst26/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260805_FezMorocco\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Royal+Palace+of+Fez&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260805_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260805_FezMorocco"},"Ssd":"20260805","Name":"FezMorocco","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6564333571","FullDateString":"5 Aug 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Owls are far more than the wide-eyed birds they appear to be. Found on every continent except Antarctica, they are known for forward-facing eyes, flat facial discs that channel sound and specialised hearing. Many species hunt in near silence thanks to soft-edged feathers. Their eyes remain fixed in their sockets, so they rely on remarkably flexible necks to scan their surroundings. From forests to deserts, owls inhabit a wide range of environments and play an important role in maintaining ecosystem balance.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.AdorableOwlet_ROW6516155898_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.AdorableOwlet_ROW6516155898_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Florida burrowing owlet, Cape Coral, Florida, United States","Copyright":"© mlorenzphotography/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260804_AdorableOwlet\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Burrowing+owl&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260804_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260804_AdorableOwlet"},"Ssd":"20260804","Name":"AdorableOwlet","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6516155898","FullDateString":"4 Aug 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Colourful luzzus—traditional Maltese fishing boats—fill the sheltered harbour of Marsaxlokk, a village on Malta's southeastern coast. Just a short drive from Valletta, the island's capital, it remains one of the country's main fishing hubs, where daily life follows the steady pace of the sea. Home to roughly 4,000 residents, the village is also widely known for its lively waterfront, especially its popular Sunday fish market, and for the steady return of boats carrying fresh catches each morning.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.BoatsMalta_ROW6191359643_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.BoatsMalta_ROW6191359643_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Colourful boats in Marsaxlokk Harbour, Malta","Copyright":"© Klubovy/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260803_BoatsMalta\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Marsaxlokk+Malta&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260803_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260803_BoatsMalta"},"Ssd":"20260803","Name":"BoatsMalta","Market":"ROW","Hash":"6191359643","FullDateString":"3 Aug 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Finland is famous for the northern lights, its high happiness rankings, saunas and thousands of lakes. Helsinki adds history, design and the Baltic Sea to that list. The story begins in 1550, when King Gustav I of Sweden founded a trading town that would become Finland's capital in 1812. Today, a walk through the city can take you from Senate Square and the white-domed Helsinki Cathedral to the sea fortress of Suomenlinna, a UNESCO World Heritage Site accessible by ferry.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.HelsinkiBlue_ROW0990097537_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.HelsinkiBlue_ROW0990097537_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Helsinki's shoreline during blue hour, Uusimaa, Finland","Copyright":"© Miemo Penttinen/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260802_HelsinkiBlue\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Helsinki&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260802_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260802_HelsinkiBlue"},"Ssd":"20260802","Name":"HelsinkiBlue","Market":"ROW","Hash":"0990097537","FullDateString":"2 Aug 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Most landscapes change over thousands of years. Volcanoes can reshape them in a matter of hours, and on Hawaii's Big Island, they continue to do just that. Recognising the scientific and natural importance of this ever-changing environment, Hawaii Volcanoes National Park was established in 1916. For more than a century, it has protected more than 1,430 square kilometres of volcanic terrain, native forests and rare wildlife.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.HawaiiLava_ROW7372744216_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.HawaiiLava_ROW7372744216_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"A series of lava flows spill into the ocean, Big Island, Hawaii, United States","Copyright":"© Ken McCurdy/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260801_HawaiiLava\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Hawaii+Volcanoes+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260801_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260801_HawaiiLava"},"S